// The lexer — and the file where *carry the subject* stops being right.
//
// `packages/wacc/src/lex.wac` is 1,281 lines and hands back a flat `i32[]` of
// `(kind, start, len, line, col)`. `@/packages/ts/src/token.wac` met the same shape in a smaller
// package, converted it to a struct, and called the flat form **a habit by the third occurrence**:
//
// > `ssz` says it is for a JS boundary that has since gone, `abi` says nothing, and this says nothing
// > either — but this one never crossed a boundary at all.
//
// That reading is right about `ts` and wrong here, and this file is why. The third occurrence had a
// reason; it is a different reason from the two it was compared against, and nothing had asked.

// **No import of `core`, and `Bytes` and `Result` are used below anyway.** That is not an oversight:
// it is §2, made visible. The name checker reports two unresolved names in this file and they are the
// measurement — a package forbidden from importing `core` still wants `core`'s two most basic types,
// and the forbidding rule is about a lambda in `Vec.fold` that this package would never call.
import { Tok, Span } from "./ast.wac";

/**
 * What a token is: a kind, and where it is.
 *
 * **`start` and `len`, not a `Bytes`** — which is a position without the thing it indexes, the shape
 * `../../../QUESTIONS.md` counts 55 of and this directory has spent three days removing. Two of the
 * removals were `@/packages/regex`'s `Span` and `@/packages/ts`'s, both deleted in favour of `Bytes`
 * on the argument that *a slice is a span that brought its subject*.
 *
 * Here the arithmetic goes the other way. A 4,000-line source is roughly **40,000 tokens**. `Bytes`
 * is `Slice<u8>` — a reference and two `i32`s — and under wasm GC a struct is a heap object, so a
 * token carrying one is **one allocation per token**: forty thousand for a file, against zero for
 * two lanes of a flat array. The span is not a failure to reach for a type. It is the only shape
 * that does not allocate per token.
 */
export struct Token { i32 kind; i32 start; i32 len; i32 line; i32 col; }

/**
 * The tokens, **and the source they index** — which is where the subject belongs.
 *
 * This is the resolution rather than a concession. The rule this directory has been applying is
 * *a position is meaningless without the thing it indexes*, and it is right; what it did not say is
 * **at which level the thing should live.** A fault is rare, so the fault carries it. A `Match` has
 * ten groups and one subject, so either works. A token stream has forty thousand and one, so the
 * subject goes on the **stream**:
 *
 * > **The subject belongs at the level where it is not repeated.**
 *
 * `Lexed` costs one field. Every token in it is `src.slice(t.start, t.start + t.len)` away from
 * being a `Bytes`, and no token pays for the reference.
 */
export struct Lexed {
  Bytes src;
  Token[] tokens;

  /** The text of one token, made when a caller wants it and not before. */
  Bytes text(const this, i32 i) {
    Token t = this.tokens[i];
    return this.src.slice(t.start, t.start + t.len);
  }
}

/** Lex, or the first thing that is not a token. */
export Result<Lexed, LexFault> lex(Bytes src) { … }

export struct BadChar { i32 at; u8 saw; }
export struct Unterminated { i32 at; string what; }
export struct BadEscape { i32 at; }
export union<BadChar, Unterminated, BadEscape> LexFault;

// ## 1. The `Token[]` is the change and the flat `i32[]` is not
//
// Two things were merged in the shipped shape and only one of them is a habit:
//
//   * **a token is five integers with no name** — `tokens[i * 5 + 2]` is a length because a comment
//     says so, and `@/packages/ts/src/token.wac`'s conversion is right about this;
//   * **a token holds a span rather than a slice** — which is the allocation argument above.
//
// `Token[]` fixes the first and keeps the second. The shipped file needs seven accessors
// (`kindAt`, `startAt`, `lenAt`, `lineAt`, `colAt`, `textAt`, `tokenCount`) to give five fields
// names; a struct gives them names and the array stays one allocation.
//
// **The hedge this file shipped with was wrong, and the answer was already written down.** It said
// `Token[]` is right *if* inline records in arrays exist — `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s open entry — and
// that entry is not open on the point that matters. It states it outright:
//
// > A WasmGC array of a struct is an array of *references* … An array's elements are packed
// > primitives (`i8`, `i16`) or references, and never inline structs. There is no third option at
// > the runtime, so the packing is a workaround for **WasmGC** rather than for wac.
//
// Confirmed against the emitter rather than the prose: `packages/wacc/src/emit.wac` writes `0x63`,
// the **nullable reference** form, for a struct element type — `if (env.isStructName(t)) { out.byte(99);
// out.i32leb(env.structType(t)); }`. So `Token[]` is 40,000 heap objects and 40,000 references, not
// conditionally but today.
//
// `Token[]` is therefore a **regression**, and the flat `i32[]` is correct. What the struct buys —
// five named fields instead of `tokens[i * 5 + 2]` — has to be bought some other way, and this file
// keeps `Token` as the *shape of a row* rather than as the element type.
//
// ## 1b. And the entry's own proposal does not reach this case either
//
// It proposes `packed struct Entry { u8 extraBits; u8 nbBits; u16 newState; }`, lowering to one
// `i32` — which fits `@/packages/zstd`'s three narrow fields and cannot fit a token's five, since
// `start` alone wants a whole word on a file of any size.
//
// So **one runtime limit produces two workarounds and only one of them has a proposal**: pack into a
// word where the fields are narrow, and parallel lanes where they are not. The second is what a
// token stream needs and what nothing here has argued for — because this directory has counted
// parallel arrays five times and called them a habit every time.
//
// ## 2. And this package cannot import the types the argument is about
//
// `Bytes` is `core`'s and `packages/wacc` **may not import `core`**. `packages/wacc/src/wvec.wac`
// exists for that rule and states it exactly:
//
// > The top rung of the ladder builds wacc — that is the whole of `./bootstrap.sh`, the only command
// > that works in a fresh clone — so every file wacc's graph reaches has to be inside the subset
// > that rung can read. `core/vec.wac` is not: its `fold` takes an `fn[U(U, T)]`, the rung below has
// > no lambdas, and it stops at the parse.
//
// So `Lexed.src` being a `Bytes` is a proposal that the bootstrap forbids today, and the reason is
// not about slices at all — it is one lambda in `Vec.fold`, three files away, in a function this
// package would never call. **A package's dependency rule is set by the weakest rung of the thing
// that builds it**, and the cost lands on a type that has nothing to do with the constraint.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`Tok` is an index into `Lexed.tokens` and nothing pairs them.** `./ast.wac` declares
// `export i32 Tok;` and every node holds one; the array it indexes is in the `Lexed` the parser had.
// Same shape as `@/packages/git/example/ignorelint.wac`'s index-into-a-`Vec` and
// `@/packages/wacc/src/decl.wac`'s *`Program` needs a path*, and here it is the **fourth** appearance
// in one compiler: `Tok`, `Span`, `Decl.at` and this.
//
// The collection-level answer above fixes it the same way — a `Tok` is meaningful relative to a
// `Lexed`, so the type that should carry the pair is the one that has one of each. Which is
// `Program { Decl[] decls; Tok path; }` from `decl.wac`, arriving at the same conclusion from the
// token end, and the two together say the compiler wants **one value that owns the source, the
// tokens and the tree**, rather than three that reference each other by integer.
//
// **`line` and `col` are computed twice and stored forever.** Every token carries them, so a 40,000
// token file stores 80,000 integers that are derivable from `start` and the source. The shipped
// lexer computes them as it goes, which is the only cheap moment — and `@/packages/json/src/parse.wac`
// computes them *later*, walking from the start of the document per fault, which it records as O(n)
// per fault and right for one. Two files, opposite trades, and the thing that decides is how many
// positions get rendered: a parser renders every token's on error and a JSON reader renders one.
//
// Nothing says which regime a program is in, and both files chose correctly by knowing their own
// caller.
